# Lecture 11B

## Gene Trees

## Chapter 11

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 17, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Read ancestry and clades from a rooted gene tree.
- Recognize rotations that preserve relationships.
- Find pairwise distances along branches of a tree.
- Build a tree from a sequence distance matrix.

=== layout: section

# Reading Gene Trees

=== layout: one-panel

# What Is a Gene Tree?

@body

- A gene tree is a hypothesis about the ancestry of gene sequences.
- Tips are the sequences being compared. Branches connect them to inferred ancestors.
- A gene tree may differ from the history of the species carrying those sequences.

=== layout: definitions

# Parts of a Rooted Tree

@body

- [*Tip*]{underline=true}: a sequence included in the comparison.
- [*Node*]{underline=true}: a branching point representing an inferred common ancestor.
- [*Branch*]{underline=true}: a connection between nodes or between a node and a tip.
- [*Root*]{underline=true}: the common ancestor of all sequences shown.
- [*Clade*]{underline=true}: an ancestor and all its descendants shown on the tree.

=== layout: two-panels

# Can You Read This Tree?

@left

![Rooted tree of four sequences](assets/lect11b_gene_tree_concepts/reading_tree.drawn.svg)

@right

- Which sequence shares a more recent common ancestor with w: x or y?
- Is the group containing w and x a clade?
- Follow the branches back to the nodes.

=== layout: two-panels

# Read the Shared Nodes

@left

![Rooted tree of four sequences](assets/lect11b_gene_tree_concepts/reading_tree.drawn.svg)

@right

- w and x share a more recent common ancestor than w and y.
- w and x form a clade: their shared node has only those two descendants.
- The root connects both pairs of sequences.

=== layout: image-comparison

