# Rotating Branches

@top-left

![First tree](assets/lect11b_gene_tree_concepts/reading_tree.drawn.svg)

@top-right

![Rotated tree](assets/lect11b_gene_tree_concepts/rotation_tree.drawn.svg)

@bottom-left

Both trees contain the w-x clade and the y-z clade.

@bottom-right

Rotating branches around a node changes the drawing, not ancestry.

=== layout: definitions

# Topology and Distance

@body

- [*Topology*]{underline=true}: the branching relationships of a tree.
- [*Branch length*]{underline=true}: on a scaled tree, the length represents the stated amount of change.
- [*Pairwise distance*]{underline=true}: the sum of branch lengths on the path connecting two tips.
- [*Unscaled tree*]{underline=true}: a tree whose drawn branch lengths need not encode distance.

=== layout: two-panels

# Tree to Pairwise Distance

@left

![Scaled tree of three sequences](assets/lect11b_gene_tree_concepts/metric_tree.drawn.svg)

@right

- p, q, and r are gene sequences. The scale is in arbitrary distance units.
- Horizontal branch lengths represent distance; vertical connectors only join branches.
- What is the distance from p to q? From p to r?

=== layout: two-panels

# Add the Branches Along the Path

@left

![Scaled tree of three sequences](assets/lect11b_gene_tree_concepts/metric_tree.drawn.svg)

@right

- p to q: 2 + 2 = 4 distance units.
- p to r: 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 distance units.
- Trace the entire path between tips, even when it passes through the root.

=== layout: section

# From Distances to a Tree

=== layout: definitions

# Distance Matrix

@body

- [*Sequence distance*]{underline=true}: a number summarizing differences between two sequences.
- [*Distance matrix*]{underline=true}: a table of the pairwise distances among sequences.
- [*Diagonal*]{underline=true}: entries comparing each sequence with itself. Values match on both sides.
- [*Cluster*]{underline=true}: a group of sequences joined during tree construction.

=== layout: one-panel

# We'll Ignore the Top Half to Avoid Confusion

@body

- The two halves of a distance matrix repeat the same pairwise distances.
- Use the lower half and skip the diagonal when finding the smallest value.
- A small distance means similar sequences, using the chosen distance measure.

=== layout: one-panel

# Building a Tree by UPGMA

@body

- Find the smallest distance and join those sequences or clusters.
- The new node height is half that distance, measured from the tips.
- Each new branch length = new height - child node height. Tips have height zero.
- For each new cluster distance, average all original pairs across the two groups.
- Repeat until one tree remains. UPGMA assumes a constant rate of change across lineages.

=== layout: section

# Four Sequences: Work It Out

=== layout: one-panel

# Four Gene Sequences

@body

- a, b, c, and d label four gene sequences.
- The matrix entries are illustrative distances in arbitrary units, not years.
- A cluster label such as ac means the sequences a and c joined together.
- For each step, predict the next join and node height before looking at the answer.

=== include: gene_tree_four_steps.djot

=== layout: section

# Choosing a Method

=== layout: one-panel

# Different Ways to Build Trees

@body

- UPGMA groups the closest clusters using average distances and assumes a constant rate.
- Neighbor-joining uses distances without requiring the same constant-rate assumption.
- Other approaches use sequence sites directly; different assumptions can lead to different trees.
- A tree is a hypothesis based on data. The ancestors are inferred, not observed.

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Shared nodes tell you ancestry; rotations preserve those relationships.
- A scaled tree gives a pairwise distance by adding branches along the path.
- A symmetric matrix gives the same pair twice; use one half to find a minimum.
- UPGMA joins clusters using average distances and a constant-rate assumption.

=== layout: theend

